{"records":[{"id":"w24t4trkv5","title":"A dataset on NFKB1/NFKB2 knockout Jurkat cells and primary CD4+ T cells generated by CRISPR/Cas9 mediated gene disruption. ","description":"Specific biological roles of non-canonical nuclear factor-kappa B (NFKB) signaling in human T cells remain unclear. Therefore, we genetically modified Jurkat leukemic cells using the clustered regularly interspaced palindromic repeat (CRISPR)/CRISPR-associated protein 9 technology to better understand the NFKB signaling mechanisms. IKAROS family zinc finger-2 levels were downregulated in NFKB2-knocked-out cells but upregulated in NFKB1-knocked-out cells. To further understand the biological roles of NFKB signaling in CD4+ T cells, NFKB1/NFKB2 loci of human primary CD4+ T cells were genetically edited using the CRISPR/CRISPR-associated protein 9 technology. Quantitative polymerase chain reaction and fluorescence-activated cell sorting revealed the similar phenotypes and gene expression profiles of NFKB1/NFKB2-knocked-out human CD4+ T cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-06T16:44:39.354Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w24t4trkv5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w24t4trkv5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yohei","last_name":"Sato","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Biotechnology","Genome Editing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7tfy4jbxp9","title":"Human gamma-delta (γδ) T cells specific for common allergens are Th1-polarized in allergic donors. Yu et al. ","description":"Gamma-delta (γδ) T cells are a bridge between innate and adaptive immunity and are involved in various immunological disorders. Here we identify and present an initial characterization of human allergen-reactive γδ T cells in peripheral blood, using an activation-induced marker assay targeting upregulated 4-1BB and CD69 expression. Broad and reproducible ex vivo allergen-reactive γδ T cell responses were detected in donors sensitized to mouse, cockroach, house dust mite, and timothy grass, as well as in non-allergic participants. The reactivity to 4 different allergen extracts was readily detected in 54.2-100% of allergic subjects (0.02-16.91% of total γδ T cell response) in a donor- and allergen-specific pattern, and abrogated by TCR-blocking.  Analysis of CD40L upregulation and intracellular cytokine staining revealed a Th1-polarized response against mouse and cockroach extract stimulation. These results support the existence of allergen-reactive γδ T cells and their possible use to rebalance dysregulated Th2 responses in allergic diseases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-17T16:42:05.485Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7tfy4jbxp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7tfy4jbxp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Esther Dawen","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gvygz8cnw9","title":"Calabrese Whole Genome CRISPR Activation library in BE(2)-C Neuroblastoma cells - NGS dataset of library population","description":"NGS data to confirm representative and balanced creation of the Calabrese whole genome activation library sets A and B in BE(2)-C neuroblastoma cells. \nHuman Calabrese CRISPR Activation Pooled Library (Set A and B) was a gift from David Root and John Doench (Addgene #1000000111).\nBE(2)-C cells from ATCC CRL-2268.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-06T14:16:14.914Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gvygz8cnw9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gvygz8cnw9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lisa","last_name":"Francomacaro","orcid":null},{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroblastoma","Genomic Library","Next Generation Sequencing","CRISPR","CRISPR Activation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jv8zfkj755","title":"miR-21 sustains CD28 signalling and low-affinity T-cell responses at the expense of self-tolerance","description":"DOI: 10.1002/cti2.1321\n\nObjective\nmiR-21 is highly expressed in iNKT and activated T cells, but its T-cell autonomous functions are poorly defined. We sought to investigate the role of miR-21 in the development and functions of T and iNKT cells, representing adaptive and innate-like populations, respectively. \nMethods\nWe studied mice with a conditional deletion of miR-21 in all mature T lymphocytes.\nResults\nThymic and peripheral T and iNKT compartments were normal in miR-21 KO mice. Upon activation in vitro, miR-21 depletion reduced T-cell survival, TH17 polarization and, remarkably, T- and iNKT-cell ability to respond to a low-affinity antigens, without altering their response to high-affinity ones. Mechanistically, miR-21 sustained CD28-dependent costimulation pathways required to lower the T-cell activation threshold, inhibiting its repressors in a positive feedback circuit, in turn increasing T cell sensitivity to antigenic stimulation and survival. Upon immunization with the low-affinity self-epitope MOG35–55, miR-21 KO mice were indeed less susceptible than WT animals to the induction of EAE, whereas they mounted normal T-cell responses against high-affinity viral epitopes generated upon LCMV infection. \nConclusion\nThe induction of T cell responses to weak antigens (signal 1) depends on CD28 costimulation (signal 2). miR-21 sustains CD28 costimulation, decreasing the T-cell activation threshold and increasing their sensitivity to antigenic stimulation and survival, broadening the immune surveillance range. This occurs at the cost of unleashing autoimmunity, resulting from the recognition of weak self-antigens by autoreactive immune responses. Thus, miR-21 fine-tunes T-cell response and self/non-self discrimination. ","source":{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-14T12:43:35.548Z","url":"https://ordr.hsr.it/datasets/jv8zfkj755","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jv8zfkj755"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maya","last_name":"Fedeli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mirela","last_name":"Kuka","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Finardi","last_name":"Annamaria","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Albano","last_name":"Francesca","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Valentina","last_name":"Viganò","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matteo","last_name":"Iannacone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Roberto","last_name":"Furlan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paolo","last_name":"Dellabona","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Giulia","last_name":"Casorati","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","MicroRNA","Lymphocyte","Flow Cytometry","Experimental Autoimmune Encephalomyelitis","T Cell Receptor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5rzpnr7w65","title":"Lipidomics analysis of primary human T cells after LXR activation with GW3965","description":"The liver X receptors (LXR) are important transcriptional regulators of cholesterol, fatty acid, and phospholipid metabolism. Here we explored the effect of LXR activation on the lipid content of primary human T cells. The dataset provided contains pmol concentrations of 701 lipid species in T cells treated with a synthetic LXR agonist (GW3965) for 36 hours. Additional tabs contain the processed data which was used to generate the figures and tables presented in the referenced publication. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-05-19T07:27:47.898Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5rzpnr7w65","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5rzpnr7w65"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kirsty","last_name":"Waddington","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ines","last_name":"Pineda-Torra","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elizabeth","last_name":"Jury","orcid":null},{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Robinson","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Agents Affecting Lipid Metabolism","Lipidomics","T Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s6mwgcm4tf","title":"T cell immune discriminants of HIV reservoir size in a pediatric cohort of vertically infected individuals","description":"Flow cytometry data are divided in 3 different panel: IA panel evaluated immune activation markers in T cells subsets, ICP panel evaluates immune checkpoint molecules in T cells subsets, GAGstim panel evaluates cytokines production and Activation Induced Molecules in T cells subsets after stimulation with GAG PTE peptides pool.\nVirology data summarize the virological measurements made in the cohort as well as time of ART initiation and age. \nMolecular data reports the raw data generated by the Fluidigm approach.\nThe flow data were used for the LASSO/Poisson regression approach to generate the signature presented in the manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-13T13:41:16.898Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s6mwgcm4tf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s6mwgcm4tf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Stefano","last_name":"Rinaldi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Human Immunodeficiency Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"js5w49wgmm","title":"nCounter immune profiling of RC17 hESC-derived neural progenitor cells (NPCs) and foetal ventral mesencephalon (fVM) dopamine cells ","description":"Here we performed targeted transcriptional analysis of immune relevant genes in RC17-human embyronic stem cells (hESCs), RC17-hESC derived neural progenitor cells (NPCs) and mature dopamine neurons (DAn), differentiated according to Nolbrant et al. 2017, with comparison to human primary foetal ventral mesencephalon. All cell types were analysed either without treatment (NT) or after 24hr exposure to 5 ng/ml interferon gamma (IFNg) stimulation. The RC17-hESC derived NPCs are currently being investigated in the STEM-PD clinical trial as a dopamine cell replacement for Parkinson's disease (NCT05635409), here we utilised the Nanostring nCounter Human Immunology V2 panel, plus an additional 20 gene codeset designed to capture additional genes related to T cell activation/inhibition and graft rejection, to measure changes in immune gene expression throughout the differentiation process from hESC to mature DAn and with comparison to previously engrafted, and well-tolerated, fVM tissue. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-08T13:36:11.423Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/js5w49wgmm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/js5w49wgmm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Annabel","last_name":"Curle","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shaline","last_name":"Fazal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoling","last_name":"He","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Roger","last_name":"Barker","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joanne","last_name":"Jones","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05t17k830","name":"UK Regenerative Medicine Platform","grant_id":"MR/S020934/1"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","grant_id":"G101558"}],"categories":["Immunology","Life Sciences","Cell Biology","Stem Cell Therapy","Dopaminergic Neuron","Cell Replacement Therapy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r49yg49ddc","title":"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses. Wang et al.","description":"Supplemental Tables S1 to S5 in Microsoft Excel format for the article \"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses\" (Wang et al., 2020):\nTable S1: CRISPR screen results. MaGECK output for positive gene enrichment analysis of SARS-CoV-2, 229E and OC43 host factor screens. Related to Figure 1. \nTable S2: Gene ontology enrichment analysis of individual CRISPR screens and network propagation clusters. Related to Figure 2.\nTable S3: Network propagation results. Related to Figure 2. \nTable S4: RNP editing efficiencies in A549-ACE2 and Huh7.5.1-ACE2/TMPRSS2 cells. Related to Figures 3 and 5.\nTable S5: Oligonucleotide sequences used in this study. Related to STAR Methods. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-25T18:59:20.104Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r49yg49ddc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r49yg49ddc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andreas","last_name":"Puschnik","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wvkhpsbbfs","title":"RNA-Seq, NChip-Seq and RIP-Seq analysis of FUBP3 in T cells","description":"The Far Upstream Element Binding Protein 3 (FUBP3) enhances HIV-1 transcription and regulates immune responses pathways in T cells:\nFUBP3 (Far Upstream Element-Binding Protein 3) has emerged as a hit factor in HIV-1 transcription, yet its molecular function and mechanisms remained unexplored. In this study, we investigate the role of FUBP3 in HIV-1 transcriptional activation, focusing on its interactions with the viral transactivator protein Tat and TAR RNA. Using a combination of transcriptomic analyses, chromatin immunoprecipitation, and biochemical assays, we demonstrate that FUBP3 enhances HIV-1 transcription in a Tat-dependent manner. FUBP3 directly binds and stabilizes TAR RNA, exhibiting a strong preference for its secondary structure, which is essential for efficient Tat function. Knockdown of FUBP3 results in significant downregulation of HIV-1 transcription and alters the expression of numerous host genes involved in T cell activation and inflammation, underscoring its role in regulating immune responses. Our findings position FUBP3 as a key player in the HIV-1 lifecycle, enhancing our understanding of the interplay between viral and host factors. This study opens avenues for future research aimed at targeting FUBP3 for therapeutic intervention in HIV-1 infection, particularly in the context of latency and reactivation. By elucidating the multifaceted roles of FUBP3, we contribute valuable insights into its potential as a therapeutic target and its implications in oncogenic and inflammatory pathways.\n\nRNA-Sequencing analysis of total RNA extracted from J-Lat 10.6, Jurkat and primary CD4+T cells upon FUBP3 depletion by shRNAmirs (shFUBP3 and shCD8B).\nRIP-Seq analysis of FUBP3-IP using the EZ-Magna RIP Kit (Millipore, #17-701) in Jurkat-D6 cells.\nNative ChIP-Seq (NChIP) analysis of RNAPII and FUBP3 in J-Lat 10.6 cells with TNFa stimulation or without stimulation (MOCK).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-12T20:32:14.258Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wvkhpsbbfs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wvkhpsbbfs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Quentin","last_name":"Gibaut","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":133252,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":133,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":133252,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132515,"restricted":68,"embargoed":669},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2252},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":675},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":469},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":426},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":397},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":328},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":267},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":223}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":130623},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1900},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":132},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":75},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":56},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":512},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":356},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":356},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":303}]}}